**Squashing** in Git means combining several commits into one. It's mostly done before a pull request: instead of ten commits like "fix", "now it works" and "typo", your branch ends up with a single commit that describes the whole change. The history is easier to read, and it's easier to revert an entire feature if you need to.

In this note I'll show you three ways to squash commits, when to use each one, and how to undo it if something goes wrong.

## Before you start

Say you're on a `feature` branch created from `main`, with a few commits:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

```
a1b2c3d fix: typo
d4e5f6a feat: form validation
9f8e7d6 feat: contact form
b6adbbc (origin/main, main) chore: initial version
```

You want the top three commits to become one, on top of `main`.

## Method 1: git rebase -i (the most common)

An interactive rebase lets you choose which commits to combine.

**1. Start the rebase** from the point where your branch left `main`:

```bash
git rebase -i main
```

If you only want to squash the last few commits, you can also say how many:

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~3
```

**2. Mark the commits to squash.** Git opens your editor with a list, oldest first:

```
pick 9f8e7d6 feat: contact form
pick d4e5f6a feat: form validation
pick a1b2c3d fix: typo
```

Leave the first one as `pick` and change the rest to `squash` (or just `s`):

```
pick 9f8e7d6 feat: contact form
squash d4e5f6a feat: form validation
squash a1b2c3d fix: typo
```

If you use `fixup` (or `f`) instead of `squash`, Git combines the commit but drops its message. Handy for "typo" commits.

**3. Save and close the editor.** Git asks for the message of the combined commit. Write it, save and close.

> If **Vim** opened: press `i` to type, `Esc` to leave insert mode, then type `:wq` and `Enter` to save and quit. To use VS Code as Git's editor instead: `git config --global core.editor "code --wait"`.

Done. Check with `git log --oneline` that you now have a single commit on top of `main`.

## Method 2: git reset --soft (the fastest)

If you want to squash **all** the commits on your branch into one, without editing a list:

```bash
git reset --soft main
git commit -m "feat: contact form with validation"
```

`git reset --soft` moves the branch back to `main` but **keeps all your changes staged**. Then you make a single commit with everything.

> Use `--soft`, never `--hard`. `git reset --hard` throws your changes away.

If your local `main` is out of date, use the exact point where your branch split off:

```bash
git reset --soft $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)
```

## Method 3: git merge --squash (when merging into main)

If you'd rather leave your branch alone and squash when you merge it:

```bash
git switch main
git merge --squash feature
git commit -m "feat: contact form with validation"
```

All the changes from `feature` land on `main` as one commit. Your `feature` branch stays as it was.

On GitHub, this is the **"Squash and merge"** button on a pull request. If your team uses it, you don't need to squash by hand.

## How to push a squash to GitHub

Methods 1 and 2 rewrite your branch's history, so a regular `git push` will be rejected. Use:

```bash
git push --force-with-lease
```

`--force-with-lease` is safer than `--force`: if someone pushed something to your branch that you don't have, the push fails instead of overwriting it.

> Never squash commits that are already on a shared branch like `main`. You'd rewrite everyone's history.

## How to undo a squash

Git remembers where your branch was before the change. Find it in the reflog:

```bash
git reflog
```

```
3c4d5e6 HEAD@{0}: rebase (finish): returning to refs/heads/feature
...
a1b2c3d HEAD@{4}: commit: fix: typo
```

And go back to that point:

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD@{4}
```

Here `--hard` is exactly what you want: you're going back to the previous state.

## Which one should I use?

| Situation | Method |
| --- | --- |
| I want to choose which commits to combine | `git rebase -i` |
| I want my whole branch as one commit | `git reset --soft` |
| I want to squash on merge, without touching my branch | `git merge --squash` or "Squash and merge" on GitHub |

One last tip: when you write the message for the combined commit, use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org). I explain how to enforce it in your project in [this note on husky and commitlint](/en/notes/how-to-configure-husky).
